; ds1302_host.loom
; Loom as the microcontroller side of a DS1302 trickle-charge timekeeping
; chip (Dallas/Maxim): single-byte reads and writes of the clock, control,
; trickle-charger and RAM registers, clock and RAM burst reads and writes,
; and write-protect handling, at a bit rate set from the data sheet's 5 V
; or 2 V limits.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). The DS1302's three-wire
; interface looks like SPI and is not: one bidirectional data line, I/O,
; that turns around in the middle of a read, CE (RST on the older data
; sheet) high to frame each transfer, every byte LSB first, the command
; byte's bit 0 selecting a read, and on a read the chip's first data bit
; driven at the falling edge of the eighth command clock. spi_master.loom
; cannot be used as it is. The program uses:
;   - a BIDIR pin with its per-pin output enable (OEP), built for buses whose
;     data line changes direction (SWD's turnaround), for I/O, push-pull
;     while the host drives it and released for the chip's bytes;
;   - OUT and IN on one-pin groups (OUTGRP, INGRP), built for parallel
;     ports, so a bit goes out from bit 0 of the shifter and comes in to bit
;     0 of a register with no branch, which keeps every clock phase the same
;     length;
;   - the deadline schedule (SETD, WAITD), built for every timed protocol,
;     for SCLK, CE and the turnaround;
;   - REV, the 16-bit bit reverse, built for the bit engine's MSB/LSB
;     conversions, to put the bits sampled first-bit-highest into an LSB-first
;     byte (as tm1637_controller.loom does).
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: CE framing and its set-up,
; hold and inactive times, the LSB-first shifting, the read turnaround, the
; burst lengths, and the write-protect sequence. Weaker than a dedicated
; peripheral: every SCLK edge costs the thread a slot, so at 50 MHz the
; fastest proved clock is 833 kHz, under half the 2.0 MHz the chip allows
; at 5 V; and SCLK stops, low, while the program waits for the host's next
; data word or for room in OUTQ (the chip is static: fCLK has no minimum).
;
; Source: DS1302 data sheet, Dallas Semiconductor, dated 070900 (14 pages);
; the Maxim 19-5420 revision the brief names could not be fetched, so its
; figure and table numbers are not given here. In the 070900 copy: the
; command byte, p.3 and Figure 2; single-byte read and write, Figure 3
; (p.6); the register map, Figure 4 (p.7, an image: only what the text says
; of it is used); read and write timing, Figures 5 and 6 (p.11); the AC
; characteristics, p.10.
;
; Pins: CE = OUT0, SCLK = OUT1, I/O = BIDIR0 (push-pull while driven; the
; chip has 40 kOhm pull-downs on all three, p.11 note 6).
;
; Turnaround rule (no contention cycle): the host drives I/O only from CE
; rising (after the inactive time tCWH, during which the chip has released
; it within tCDZ) to CE falling, and on a read only up to the eighth command
; clock: it releases I/O one tick after that clock's rise (after the chip's
; hold time tCDH has run) and one tick before its fall, the edge on which
; the chip starts to drive (p.3: "the first data bit to be transmitted occurs
; on the first falling edge after the last bit of the command byte"). It
; takes I/O back only at the next transfer, after CE has been low for tCWH.
;
; Host words (FIFO space, thread 0). INQ, one operation per word:
;   bit 15 = 1, a transfer: bits 15:8 the command byte (p.3: bit 7 = 1, bit
;     6 RAM, bits 5:1 the address, bit 0 read), bits 7:0 its argument.
;     A single write (address 0..30): the argument is the data byte.
;     A single read: the argument is ignored.
;     A burst (address 31: BEH/BFH clock, FEH/FFH RAM): the argument is the
;       byte count, 0 meaning the whole burst (8 for the clock, 31 for the
;       RAM). A burst write's bytes follow in INQ, two per word, the first
;       in bits 7:0; they are popped as they go out.
;   bit 15 = 0, a setting, bits 14:12 which:
;     0x100r  the clock rate: r = 0 the 5 V rate (TICK_INT 12, the reset
;             choice), r = 1 the 2 V rate (TICK_INT 28). See "Timing".
;             ds1302_device.loom, a software chip, needs the 2 V rate.
;     0x200g  write-protect handling: g = 1 wraps every write except one to
;             the control register in "8EH 00H" before it (clear WP, bit 7
;             of the control register, p.4) and "8EH 80H" after it (set it
;             again); a clock burst write is not followed by 8EH 80H, since
;             its eighth byte is the control register itself. g = 0 sends
;             each write as it is (the reset choice).
; OUTQ: a read gives one word per byte, command byte in bits 15:8 and the
; byte read in 7:0; a write or a setting gives its own word back when it is
; done. The PUSHes block while OUTQ is full (SCLK waits low).
;
; Timing (p.10, the AC table; 2.0 V and 5.0 V figures). One tick is U
; clocks; a bit is LOW = 3 ticks and HIGH = 2. SCLK falls one slot after
; its deadline and rises two slots after its own (I/O is sampled in the
; slot between), so inside a byte SCLK is low 3U + 4 clocks and high 2U - 4:
;   5 V rate, U = 12 (50 MHz): tCL 800 ns (>= 250), tCH 400 ns (>= 250),
;     1.2 us, 833 kHz (<= 2.0 MHz); 2 V rate, U = 28: tCL 1.76 us (>= 1.0),
;     tCH 1.04 us (>= 1.0), 2.8 us, 357 kHz (<= 500 kHz). U is a multiple
;     of the 4-clock slot, so every edge is late on its deadline by the same
;     amount and the phases are exact (SEMANTICS 2). Between bytes the low
;     phase is longer (each byte re-anchors its schedule); SCLK is static,
;     so that costs only time.
;   I/O changes a few slots after a fall, at least 480 ns (5 V) and 1.28
;     us (2 V) before the rise (tDC >= 50 and 200 ns), and holds for the
;     whole high phase (tCDH >= 70 and 280 ns); the eighth clock of a read's
;     command holds it one tick, 240 ns and 560 ns, then lets go. The chip's
;     bit is sampled one slot before the rise, about 660 ns and 1.62 us
;     after the fall, against its delay tCDD <= 200 ns and 800 ns.
;   CE to the first rise: CC + LOW = 8 ticks and a few slots, 2.1 us and
;     4.2 us (tCC >= 1 us and 4 us); the last fall to CE falling: HOLD = 2
;     ticks and slots, 1.2 us and 1.7 us (tCCH >= 60 and 240 ns, taken from
;     the last edge); CE low for CWH = 8 ticks or more, 1.9 us and 4.5 us
;     (tCWH >= 1 us and 4 us).
; The schedule is proved for ticks of 12 clocks or more.
;
; Memory: 167 words. The main loop, 110, in thread 0's quarter
; (0x000..0x06D) and, from `guarded` on, the subroutines, 56, in thread 3's
; (0x180..0x1B7), which no program of the pair runs from; the write-protect
; setting in the last word of the memory (GUARD, 0x1FF). With
; ds1302_device.loom (thread 1: 0x080..0x168, and 0x1C0..0x1CB between
; these subroutines and GUARD) the two load together; threads 2 and 3 must
; not be started.
;
; Registers: r0 transmit shifter, r1 bit count, r2 the command byte, r3 the
; byte received and the result, r4 bytes still to go, r5 scratch, r6 the
; burst write's data word, r7 the operation word (and the .csr scratch
; before the first POP).

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   CE = OUT0, SCLK = OUT1, IO = BIDIR0
        .equ    U5 = 12                 ; the 5 V tick, clocks (50 MHz)
        .equ    U2 = 28                 ; the 2 V tick
        .equ    LOW = 3                 ; SCLK low, ticks
        .equ    HIGH = 2                ; SCLK high, ticks
        .equ    CC = 5                  ; CE high to the first bit's low phase
        .equ    HOLD = 2                ; the last fall to CE low
        .equ    CWH = 8                 ; CE low between transfers
        .equ    WCTRL = 0x8E            ; write the control register (p.4)
        .equ    WCLKB = 0xBE            ; clock burst write
        .equ    WP = 0x80               ; the control register's write protect
        .equ    GUARD = 0x1FF           ; the write-protect setting, 0 or 1

start:  .csr    OUTGRP, (1 << 5) | pin(IO)   ; OUT drives I/O from bit 0
        .csr    INGRP, (1 << 5) | pin(IO)    ; IN reads I/O into bit 0
        SETP    CE, 0
        SETP    SCLK, 0                 ; SCLK low before CE rises (p.3)
        OEP     IO, 0                   ; released: the chip's pull-down
        .csr    TICK_INT, U5            ; the 5 V rate; also the checker's .tick
        LDI     r7, 0
        CSRW    TICK_FRAC, r7

cmd:    POP     r7                      ; next operation (blocks while INQ is empty)
        SETD    0                       ; re-anchor after the unbounded POP
        MOV     r2, r7
        SHRI    r2, 15
        BZ      setting                 ; bit 15 clear: a setting
        MOV     r2, r7
        SHRI    r2, 8                   ; the command byte
        MOV     r5, r2
        ANDI    r5, 1
        BNZ     read
        CALL    guarded                 ; Z clear: wrap this write
        BZ      w_go
        LDI     r0, WCTRL
        LDI     r4, 0
        CALL    wone                    ; 8EH 00H: clear WP first
w_go:   MOV     r4, r7
        SHLI    r4, 8
        SHRI    r4, 8                   ; the argument
        CALL    isburst
        BZ      bwrite
        MOV     r0, r2
        CALL    wone                    ; command, data
        JMP     w_end
bwrite: CALL    begin
        MOV     r0, r2
        LDI     r1, 8
        CALL    byte                    ; the burst command
bw_w:   POP     r6                      ; two data bytes (SCLK waits low)
        MOV     r0, r6
        LDI     r1, 8
        CALL    byte                    ; bits 7:0
        SUBI    r4, 1
        BZ      bw_end
        MOV     r0, r6
        SWAP    r0, r0
        LDI     r1, 8
        CALL    byte                    ; bits 15:8
        SUBI    r4, 1
        BNZ     bw_w
bw_end: CALL    end
w_end:  CALL    guarded
        BZ      ack
        LDI     r5, WCLKB
        CMP     r2, r5
        BZ      ack                     ; a clock burst wrote the control byte
        LDI     r0, WCTRL
        LDI     r4, WP
        CALL    wone                    ; 8EH 80H: set WP again
ack:    PUSH    r7                      ; the operation word back: done
        JMP     cmd

; A read, single or burst: the command with the turnaround, then the bytes.
read:   MOV     r4, r7
        SHLI    r4, 8
        SHRI    r4, 8
        CALL    isburst
        BZ      r_go                    ; a burst: r4 bytes
        LDI     r4, 1                   ; a single byte
r_go:   CALL    begin
        MOV     r0, r2
        LDI     r1, 7
        CALL    byte                    ; command bits 0..6
        SETD    0
        OUT     r0                      ; bit 7 (r0 = 1 now)
        WAITD   LOW
        SETP    SCLK, 1                 ; the eighth rise: the chip takes bit 7
        WAITD   1
        OEP     IO, 0                   ; turnaround: tCDH has run, the fall is a tick off
        WAITD   1
        SETP    SCLK, 0                 ; the chip drives its bit 0 from here
r_byte: LDI     r1, 8
        CALL    byte
        MOV     r5, r2
        SWAP    r5, r5
        OR      r3, r3, r5              ; command byte << 8 | the byte
        PUSH    r3                      ; blocks while OUTQ is full: SCLK waits low
        SUBI    r4, 1
        BNZ     r_byte
        CALL    end
        JMP     cmd

; A setting: the clock rate or the write-protect handling.
setting: MOV    r5, r7
        SHRI    r5, 12
        CMPI    r5, 1
        BZ      rate
        CMPI    r5, 2
        BNZ     ack                     ; not a setting: given back unchanged
        MOV     r5, r7
        ANDI    r5, 1
        MOV16   r3, GUARD
        ST      r5, r3, 0
        JMP     ack
rate:   MOV     r5, r7
        ANDI    r5, 1
        LDI     r3, U5                  ; LDI leaves the flags alone
        BZ      r_set
        LDI     r3, U2
r_set:  CSRW    TICK_INT, r3            ; a slower tick: the proof at 12 holds
        JMP     ack

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The spill: the subroutines sit in thread 3's quarter, 0x180 on (see
; "Memory" above). Thread 3 must not be started with this image.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        .org    0x180

; guarded: Z clear if the write of command r2 is to be wrapped in 8EH 00H and
; 8EH 80H (handling on, and not the control register itself).
guarded: MOV16  r5, GUARD
        LD      r5, r5, 0
        TEST    r5, r5
        BZ      g_ret                   ; handling off: Z set
        LDI     r5, WCTRL
        CMP     r2, r5                  ; Z set for 8EH
g_ret:  RET

; isburst: Z set if command r2 addresses 31 (a burst); then r4 = 0 becomes
; the whole burst, 8 bytes for the clock, 31 for the RAM (p.4, p.5).
isburst: MOV    r5, r2
        ANDI    r5, 0x3E
        CMPI    r5, 0x3E
        BNZ     ib_ret
        TEST    r4, r4
        BNZ     ib_z
        LDI     r4, 8
        MOV     r5, r2
        SHRI    r5, 6
        ANDI    r5, 1                   ; bit 6: the RAM
        BZ      ib_z
        LDI     r4, 31
ib_z:   CMP     r4, r4                  ; Z set: a burst
ib_ret: RET

; wone: a single-byte write, command r0, data r4.
wone:   CALL    begin
        LDI     r1, 8
        CALL    byte
        MOV     r0, r4
        LDI     r1, 8
        CALL    byte
        CALL    end
        RET

; begin: CE rises with SCLK low; the host drives I/O.
begin:  SETD    0
        SETP    CE, 1
        OEP     IO, 1
        WAITD   CC                      ; with LOW: tCC before the first rise
        RET

; byte: r1 bits of r0, LSB first, out on I/O (ignored while I/O is
; released) and the bits on I/O in; returns the r1 = 8 bits received in r3
; and SCLK low. Every bit: I/O set after the fall, sampled one slot
; before the rise.
byte:   SETD    0                       ; a fresh schedule: SCLK is low
        LDI     r3, 0
bloop:  OUT     r0                      ; I/O = the next bit
        SHRI    r0, 1
        WAITD   LOW                     ; end of the low phase
        IN      r5                      ; sample I/O into bit 0
        SETP    SCLK, 1                 ; the rise: the chip takes I/O
        WAITD   HIGH
        SETP    SCLK, 0                 ; the fall: a reading chip drives here
        ADD     r3, r3, r3
        OR      r3, r3, r5              ; first bit received ends in bit 7
        DJNZ    r1, bloop
        REV     r3, r3                  ; bit i to bit 15 - i
        SWAP    r3, r3                  ; the byte, first bit in bit 0
        RET

; end: CE falls after the hold, I/O released, CE low for tCWH.
end:    SETD    0
        WAITD   HOLD
        SETP    CE, 0                   ; the chip releases I/O (tCDZ)
        OEP     IO, 0
        WAITD   CWH
        RET

        .org    GUARD
        .word   0                       ; write-protect handling off at load
